  [1mApproaches for the store[0m (everything else is locked by your answers):

  - [1mA — Accessor with a versioned record.[0m The module exposes [38;5;153mset()[39m, [38;5;153mget()[39m, [38;5;153mclear()[39m. It persists one JSON envelope under
    a single [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m key: [38;5;153m{ v: 1, userId, username, loginAt }[39m. Anything unparseable or carrying an unknown [38;5;153mv[39m is
    treated as "no user" and cleared, so a bad write can never wedge the app. The storage object is a constructor/init
    argument defaulting to [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, which is what makes it testable under [38;5;153mnode:test[39m. Moderate complexity.
  - [1mB — Observable store.[0m A, plus [38;5;153msubscribe(fn)[39m so future forms re-render when identity changes. Buys reactivity; costs
    listener lifecycle, teardown, and a bigger API surface to get right. It's only worth it if the later forms need to
    [3mreact[0m to identity changing rather than read it when they submit. Moderate-to-high.
  - [1mC — Bare key/value.[0m Write the [38;5;153muserId[39m string straight to a [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m key; no envelope, no module state, maybe no
    module at all. Smallest possible change. But there's nowhere to put [38;5;153musername[39m or [38;5;153mloginAt[39m later without a migration
    over data already in users' browsers, and no way to tell corrupt data from absent data. Trivial.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. C is tempting given how small this app is, but you told me other forms will consume this later, and the
  envelope is the cheap insurance that makes adding a field later a non-event rather than a migration. B's reactivity
  is speculative — nothing in the app today re-renders on identity change, and [38;5;153msubscribe[39m is easy to add to A later
  without touching the stored format.
